Operators and Functions

1. arr.push()
=> Adds element at the end of the array and return the new length of the array. Mutates the original array
2. arr.pop()
=> Removes the last element of the array and return it. Mutates the original array
3. arr.shift()
=> Removes the first element of the array and returns the removed element.Takes more time than pop
as first element is removed and rest elements are shifted to the begining.
4. arr.unshift()
=>unshift() adds one or more elements to the beginning of an array and returns the new length. It also mutatates the original array
It adds the elements in the order you pass them.
5. arr.slice(start, end)
=> slice() is used to extract a portion of an array without modifying the original array.Start index is is included but end is not
=> If just one argument is provided like slice(2), it means start from 2nd index and go till the end (end is included)
=> If no arg is provided like slice(), entire array is sliced to a new array. This way is used to make shallow copies of the array
=> Array indexex starts from 0 and goes from start to end. But it can be represent from end to start using negative indexes
where end is represented by -1 and the other element like -2 -3 so on till the start
so slice(-2) means slice the array from second last element to the end
6. arr.splice()
splice() is used to add, remove, or replace elements in an array.
array.splice(start, deleteCount, item1, item2, ...)
deleteCount = 0  → ADD
deleteCount > 0  → REMOVE
deleteCount > 0 + new items → REPLACE
splice() returns an array containing the removed elements.
